<section class="section reveal" id="why-functions">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Why Functions Matter</h2>

  <p>In the real world, you will never write a single script that solves an entire problem. Professional AI systems are composed of hundreds or thousands of functions, each doing one thing exceptionally well. Functions are the building blocks that allow you to:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eliminate Repetition:</strong> Write code once, use it everywhere. If you copy-paste code three times, that is a sign you need a function.</li>
    <li><strong>Enable Collaboration:</strong> When your function has a clear interface (inputs and outputs), teammates can use your code without understanding the implementation details.</li>
    <li><strong>Simplify Testing:</strong> Isolated functions are easy to test. You can verify each function works correctly before integrating it into a larger system.</li>
    <li><strong>Facilitate Debugging:</strong> When something breaks, functions help you isolate the problem. Is it the data, the logic, or the output format? Functions answer these questions.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>A function is like a recipe. The ingredients are your inputs (parameters). The cooking instructions are your code. The finished dish is your output (return value). When you need the same dish again, you don't rewrite the recipe — you just follow it again. In code, this is called DRY: Don't Repeat Yourself.</p>
  </div>

