<section class="section reveal" id="function-basics">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
  <h2>Function Definition and Syntax</h2>

  <p>A Python function is defined using the <code>def</code> keyword. The basic structure is simple: specify inputs (parameters), describe what the function does (body), and specify outputs (return statement). Let us break this down:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Basic function structure</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">calculate_accuracy</span>(<span class="va">correct</span>, <span class="va">total</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Calculate classification accuracy.

    Args:
        correct (int): Number of correct predictions
        total (int): Total number of predictions

    Returns:
        float: Accuracy as a decimal (0.0 to 1.0)
    """</span>
    <span class="va">accuracy</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">correct</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">total</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">accuracy</span>

<span class="cm"># Call the function with arguments</span>
<span class="va">acc</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">calculate_accuracy</span>(<span class="nm">950</span>, <span class="nm">1000</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Model accuracy: </span>{<span class="va">acc</span>:.1%}<span class="st">"</span>)  <span class="cm"># Model accuracy: 95.0%</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> We define a function named <code>calculate_accuracy</code> that takes two parameters: <code>correct</code> and <code>total</code>. The docstring (triple-quoted string) explains what the function does, its parameters, and return value. Inside the function, we calculate the accuracy and return it. When we call the function with arguments, Python executes the body and returns the result.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Parameters vs Arguments</h3>
  <p>These terms are often confused, but they have distinct meanings:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Parameters:</strong> The variable names in the function definition (e.g., <code>correct</code> and <code>total</code> in the example above)</li>
    <li><strong>Arguments:</strong> The actual values you pass when calling the function (e.g., <code>950</code> and <code>1000</code> in the example above)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Default Parameters</h3>
  <p>Functions often have sensible default values. Instead of forcing callers to specify every parameter, you can provide defaults:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">normalize_data</span>(<span class="va">data</span>, <span class="va">mean</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="va">std</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>):
    <span class="st">"""Normalize data using z-score normalization.

    Args:
        data (list): Values to normalize
        mean (float): Target mean (default: 0)
        std (float): Target standard deviation (default: 1)
    """</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> [(<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">-</span> <span class="va">mean</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">std</span> <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">x</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">data</span>]

<span class="cm"># Call with defaults</span>
<span class="va">normalized</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">normalize_data</span>([<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">20</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>])

<span class="cm"># Call with custom values</span>
<span class="va">normalized</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">normalize_data</span>([<span class="nm">10</span>, <span class="nm">20</span>, <span class="nm">30</span>], <span class="va">mean</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">50</span>, <span class="va">std</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">10</span>)</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> The function <code>normalize_data</code> has default values for <code>mean</code> and <code>std</code>. If you don't specify them, Python uses the defaults (0 and 1). If you do specify them, your values override the defaults. This flexibility makes your functions powerful without requiring callers to remember every parameter.</p>
  </div>

