<section class="section reveal" id="scope">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced Concepts</span>
  <h2>Understanding Variable Scope</h2>

  <p>One of the most common bugs in Python comes from misunderstanding scope. Scope determines which parts of your code can access a variable. In Python, there are four scope levels, remembered by the acronym <strong>LEGB</strong>:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Local (L):</strong> Variables inside the current function</li>
    <li><strong>Enclosing (E):</strong> Variables in outer functions (for nested functions)</li>
    <li><strong>Global (G):</strong> Variables defined at the module level</li>
    <li><strong>Built-in (B):</strong> Python's built-in functions and constants like <code>len()</code>, <code>print()</code>, <code>True</code></li>
  </ul>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine a house with rooms. Variables in your bedroom (local) are only accessible to you in that room. Variables in the living room (global) are accessible to everyone in the house. If you try to access something from the kitchen when you're in your bedroom, you have to go get it — and you can't borrow it without permission.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Global scope</span>
<span class="va">model_name</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"ResNet50"</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train_model</span>():
    <span class="cm"># Local scope</span>
    <span class="va">learning_rate</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.001</span>
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">model_name</span>)          <span class="cm"># OK: can access global</span>
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">learning_rate</span>)    <span class="cm"># OK: it's local</span>

<span class="fn">train_model</span>()
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">learning_rate</span>)      <span class="cm"># ERROR: learning_rate is local to train_model()</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> <code>model_name</code> is global, so any function can see it. <code>learning_rate</code> is defined inside <code>train_model()</code>, making it local to that function. Once the function returns, the local variable is gone. Trying to access it outside causes a <code>NameError</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Modifying Global Variables</h3>
  <p>If you want to modify a global variable from inside a function, use the <code>global</code> keyword:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="va">epoch</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0</span>

<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train_step</span>():
    <span class="kw">global</span> <span class="va">epoch</span>
    <span class="va">epoch</span> <span class="op">+=</span> <span class="nm">1</span>
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Epoch </span>{<span class="va">epoch</span>}<span class="st">"</span>)

<span class="fn">train_step</span>()  <span class="cm"># Epoch 1</span>
<span class="fn">train_step</span>()  <span class="cm"># Epoch 2</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">epoch</span>)   <span class="cm"># 2</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> The <code>global</code> keyword tells Python that we want to modify the global <code>epoch</code> variable, not create a local one. Without this keyword, Python would create a new local variable instead. Notice how the global <code>epoch</code> actually increases with each call.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In ML Training Loops:</strong> Global variables that track training state (epoch, step, best loss) can become confusing and error-prone. Best practice: avoid relying on global state. Instead, return values or use classes to encapsulate state. This makes code more testable and predictable.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Python resolves names using LEGB scope: Local → Enclosing → Global → Built-in</li>
      <li>Variables defined in a function are local to that function</li>
      <li>Use <code>global</code> keyword only when absolutely necessary — prefer returning values instead</li>
      <li>Minimizing global state makes code easier to test and debug</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

