<section class="section reveal" id="advanced-patterns">
  <span class="section-label">Best Practices</span>
  <h2>Advanced Patterns and Documentation</h2>

  <p>Production AI code goes beyond basic functions. You need to handle edge cases, document assumptions, and use patterns that make code robust and maintainable. This section covers practices used in professional codebases.</p>

  <h3>Type Hints</h3>
  <p>Type hints tell readers (and tools) what types your function expects and returns. This catches bugs early:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">compute_metrics</span>(<span class="va">predictions</span>: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">float</span>], <span class="va">targets</span>: <span class="fn">list</span>[<span class="fn">int</span>]) <span class="op">-></span> <span class="fn">dict</span>:
    <span class="st">"""Calculate precision, recall, and F1 score.

    Args:
        predictions: Model output probabilities [0, 1]
        targets: Ground truth labels [0, 1]

    Returns:
        Dictionary with 'precision', 'recall', 'f1' keys
    """</span>
    <span class="cm"># Implementation here</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="st">"precision"</span>: <span class="nm">0.92</span>, <span class="st">"recall"</span>: <span class="nm">0.89</span>, <span class="st">"f1"</span>: <span class="nm">0.90</span>}</code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Type hints use colons for parameters and <code>-></code> for the return type. They don't enforce types at runtime in Python 3.9, but they make code clearer and allow static analysis tools to catch bugs before you run your code.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Input Validation</h3>
  <p>Production code validates inputs to fail early with clear error messages:</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train_model</span>(<span class="va">epochs</span>: <span class="fn">int</span>, <span class="va">learning_rate</span>: <span class="fn">float</span>) <span class="op">-></span> <span class="kw">None</span>:
    <span class="cm"># Validate inputs</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">epochs</span> <span class="op"><=</span> <span class="nm">0</span>:
        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="st">"epochs must be positive"</span>)
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="kw">not</span> (<span class="nm">0</span> <span class="op"><</span> <span class="va">learning_rate</span> <span class="op"><</span> <span class="nm">1</span>):
        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="fn">ValueError</span>(<span class="st">"learning_rate must be between 0 and 1"</span>)

    <span class="cm"># Safe to proceed — inputs are valid</span>
    <span class="va">results</span> <span class="op">=</span> []
    <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">epoch</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="va">epochs</span>):
        <span class="cm"># Training logic</span>
        <span class="va">loss</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">0.5</span> <span class="op">/</span> (<span class="va">epoch</span> <span class="op">+</span> <span class="nm">1</span>)
        <span class="va">results</span>.<span class="fn">append</span>(<span class="va">loss</span>)
    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">results</span></code></pre>
  <div class="code-explain">
    <p><strong>What is happening here:</strong> Before executing the function body, we validate that epochs is positive and learning_rate is in the valid range. If validation fails, we raise an exception with a clear message. This catches bugs immediately, rather than letting bad data corrupt your model.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="infographic reveal">
    <div class="infographic-title">Production Function Checklist</div>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>✅ Good Production Code</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Clear function name describing what it does</li>
          <li>Docstring with Args, Returns, Raises sections</li>
          <li>Type hints for all parameters and return</li>
          <li>Input validation with clear error messages</li>
          <li>Handles edge cases (empty inputs, None, etc.)</li>
          <li>Returns consistently typed values</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-card">
        <h5>❌ Problematic Code</h5>
        <ul>
          <li>Vague names like <code>process()</code> or <code>helper()</code></li>
          <li>No docstring or outdated docstring</li>
          <li>No type hints</li>
          <li>Assumes inputs are valid, crashes on bad data</li>
          <li>Doesn't handle edge cases</li>
          <li>Return type varies (sometimes list, sometimes None)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Type hints document expected types and enable static analysis</li>
      <li>Validate inputs early with clear, informative error messages</li>
      <li>Write comprehensive docstrings following standard format</li>
      <li>Handle edge cases (empty, None, out-of-range values) gracefully</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

